## The Safe Wrapper Pattern

### Unsafe Internals, Safe API

```xiom
// Internal: raw pointer with unsafe operations
pub type Buffer = {
  ptr: *Byte;    // unsafe: raw pointer
  len: Int;      // unsafe: must track manually
  cap: Int;
}
  invariant: self.ptr != null;
  invariant: self.len <= self.cap;

// Safe API — all contracts checked, no UB possible for callers
impl Buffer {
  fn new(size: Int) -> Buffer
    requires: size > 0;
    ensures: result.ptr != null;
    ensures: result.len == 0;
    ensures: result.cap == size;
  {
    let ptr = alloc(size);  // unsafe call
    return Buffer{ ptr: ptr, len: 0, cap: size };
  }

  fn write(self: &mut Self, data: *Byte, n: Int)
    requires: data != null;
    requires: self.len + n <= self.cap;
  {
    // unsafe copy — but safe because contracts guarantee bounds
    copy(self.ptr + self.len, data, n);
    self.len = self.len + n;
  }

  fn read(self: &Self, index: Int) -> Byte
    requires: index >= 0;
    requires: index < self.len;
    ensures: result == self.ptr[index];
  {
    return self.ptr[index];
  }
}
```

### The Pattern
1. The type holds unsafe data (raw pointers)
2. An invariant guarantees core safety
3. Every method has contracts preventing UB
4. Callers never see or touch the unsafe internals

### Layered Safety
```
Caller → Safe API (contracts) → Unsafe Implementation → Hardware/FFI
```

If a contract violation occurs, it traps at the Safe API layer — never reaching the unsafe code.

